        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(proxy-capture): guard body-less arrayBuffer reads against oversized responses",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\r\n\r\nFixes an issue where `readCapturedResponseBodyBounded` in the proxy capture runtime would buffer the full response body through `arrayBuffer()` before checking the byte cap when the cloned response lacks a streaming body. A response with an oversized declared `content-length` would still be fully allocated in memory before the post-read check discarded it, violating the function's bounded-read contract.\r\n\r\n## Why This Change Was Made\r\n\r\nThe body-less fallback path (reachable with non-standard Response clones) now checks the `content-length` header before calling `arrayBuffer()`. When the declared length exceeds `maxBytes`, the function returns `{ truncated: true }` immediately without materializing the body. The stream path (normal ReadableStream clones) already enforced the cap correctly via chunked reading with `reader.cancel()`.\r\n\r\n## User Impact\r\n\r\nOperators running the debug proxy capture with large or hostile provider responses get the same OOM guard in the body-less path that already exists in the stream path. No config or API surface changes.\r\n\r\n## Evidence\r\n\r\n**Environment:** Node.js v24, real `readCapturedResponseBodyBounded` exported function. Body-less fallback tests use mock clones (`body: null`) to force the `!body` branch; stream-path tests use real `Response` objects.\r\n\r\n**After fix — body-less fallback tests (mock clone, 3 cases):**\r\n\r\n```\r\n[case 1] content-length exceeds cap: returns truncated, arrayBuffer() NOT called\r\n  ok: truncated\r\n  ok: buffer empty\r\n  ok: arrayBuffer spy not called\r\n[case 2] content-length within cap: falls through to arrayBuffer(), returns body\r\n  ok: not truncated\r\n  ok: body matches\r\n  ok: arrayBuffer called once\r\n[case 3] missing content-length, within cap: falls through to arrayBuffer()\r\n  ok: not truncated\r\n  ok: body matches\r\n  ok: arrayBuffer called once\r\n```\r\n\r\n**After fix — stream path tests (real Response, 3 cases):**\r\n\r\n```\r\n[case 4] content-length within cap: returns body\r\n  ok: not truncated | ok: body matches\r\n[case 5] missing content-length, within cap: returns body\r\n  ok: not truncated | ok: body matches\r\n[case 6] stream body within cap: returns body (regression guard)\r\n  ok: not truncated | ok: correct size\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Negative control (content-length precheck removed, same tests, same environment):**\r\n\r\n```\r\nFAIL: skips body read when content-length exceeds cap (body-less fallback)\r\nTypeError: The first argument must be of type string or an instance of Buffer...\r\n  → arrayBuffer() was called (returned undefined), guard is proven load-bearing\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Focused tests:**\r\n\r\n```\r\nnode scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/proxy-capture/runtime.test.ts\r\nTests  14 passed (14)  (8 existing + 6 new)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Lint/format:** `oxfmt --check` and `oxlint` clean on changed files.\r\n",
